close_reason: "Implemented recorded-node structural ratchets independent of placeholders, with durable compatibility, public contracts, docs, and repair-versus-erosion negative controls."
body: "## Measured this session, in order\n\nStarting state: `pm graph audit` reported 11 findings across four legacy codes; `pm assurance run tracker-context-quality --trigger ci` reported `verdict: pass`. Graph profile: 2,458 nodes, of which 3 were assembly-materialized placeholders for dangling references (`missing_nodes: 3`), and 12,116 edges.\n\nExecuted exactly the remediation the audit prescribes, on three of its four codes:\n\n- 8 duplicated `child` spellings collapsed onto the canonical `parent` edge each child already stored (`legacy_duplicate_edge` 8 to 0);\n- 3 dangling references removed: one dependency row to an item that does not exist, and two legacy free-text `blocked_by` scalars that were never item ids (`missing_reference_terminal` 3 to 0);\n- 98 retired `no-active-blocker` sentinel scalars cleared (`legacy_no_blocker_sentinel` 98 to 0).\n\nResulting state: 8 findings, all of one remaining code. Graph profile: 2,455 nodes, `missing_nodes: 0`, 12,105 edges. `pm assurance run tracker-context-quality --trigger ci` now reports `verdict: block`, `exit_code: 1`, on `graph-nodes-floor`: observed 2455, floor 2456, distance -1, enforcement `block`.\n\nThe three nodes that disappeared are exactly the three placeholders. The corpus of real recorded items did not change.\n\n## The declaration says so in its own words\n\n`pm assurance show measurement graph-nodes` describes its source as \"All indexed graph nodes including missing placeholders\" and reads `profile.nodes` from the audit.\n\n`pm assurance show assertion graph-nodes-floor` carries `floor: 2456` and this negative control:\n\n```\nnegative_control:\n  cases[2]{observed,expected}:\n    2456,pass\n    2455,fail\n```\n\nThere are 2,455 real items. The negative control therefore states that a corpus containing every recorded item and zero dangling references must fail. The floor is satisfiable only while at least one reference points at an item that does not exist.\n\n## Why this is a second instance, not a restatement\n\npm-9gzr4r owns the edge-count floor being denominated over a population that includes the edges the graph itself calls redundant. That is an argument about redundancy. This is a different measurement (`graph-nodes`), a different assertion owner (pm-o2kc), and a different mechanism: placeholder materialization during assembly, which is a deliberate feature so bounded traversals can explain absent work rather than silently dropping it. The feature is correct. Ratcheting a floor over its output is what is wrong.\n\n## The edge floor blocks the fourth code as well\n\n`graph-edges-floor` is 12103 with `enforcement: block`; observed is 12105, so headroom is 2 edges. The remaining legacy code is 8 terminal ordering cycles across 49 items, whose root cause is 26 holders that each store a legacy scalar `blocked_by: X` together with a contradicting structured row `X, blocks`. Removing those 26 inverted rows takes edges to 12,079 and fails the floor by 24.\n\nSo all four of pm-hnc9w7's remediation criteria are individually blocked by a total-count floor. The finding-count ceilings in the same registry behave correctly and reward the same cleanup: `graph-finding-legacy-duplicate-edge` (ceiling 8), `legacy-no-blocker-sentinel` (98), `missing-reference-terminal` (3) all moved to 0 and still pass. Two families of declarations in one registry disagree about whether cleanup is progress.\n\n## Boundary\n\nThe fix is the denominator, not the numbers. Raising or lowering a floor by hand is the erosion this class is supposed to detect, so the repair must make removal-of-meaningless and removal-of-meaningful structurally distinguishable at the gate."
